{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Command Aaron.</b> They said, their memory for blessing, in Torat Kohanim (see Kiddushin 29a): there is no צַו ('command') other than an exhortation, for immediately and for the generations. Rabbi Shimon said: all the more does the verse need to exhort in every place where there is חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס ('shortcoming of the purse'), end of quote. Now, for the first Tanna there is here an exhortation, because the laws of the olah on the altar at night are unusually detailed, which is not the case for any other offering; therefore he exhorted him for immediately — meaning, that this procedure should be practiced at that time — as they learned in Torat Kohanim from the passage of the lamps, where it is written (Leviticus 24:2): ״צַו אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְיִקְחוּ אֵלֶיךָ״ etc., and it is written (Numbers 8:3): ״וַיַּעַשׂ כֵּן אַהֲרֹן״. For the generations — meaning, that the laws of the olah should be practiced according to their written form for the generations. For they said, their memory for blessing (in the Pesikta on the verse אֶת קָרְבָּנִי לַחְמִי, Numbers 28:2), and this is their language: Because Israel would say, in the past the journeys were practiced and the daily offerings were practiced; the journeys ceased — the daily offerings ceased, end of quote. We see, then, that there is room to err into thinking that they should not be practiced for the generations." "As for the חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס ('shortcoming of the purse') that Rabbi Shimon speaks of, the explanations have multiplied: some say it is not literally a shortcoming of the purse, but the same applies to any multiplication of bother; and some say that the priest was idle from his work all night, and that is a חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס; and some say חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס refers to Israel, for the limbs of the olah are burned on the altar and they derive no benefit from them; and some say חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס refers to the priest, for he would prefer to offer other offerings in which he has a share, unlike the olah, which is entirely consumed and from which he has only the skin. And all these explanations are far-fetched in my eyes." "<b>Perhaps</b> it may be seen that it refers to the arrangement of wood, about which they said, their memory for blessing (Yoma 45a): three arrangements of fire were there, etc. — the third has nothing on it, but only to fulfill ״אֵשׁ תָּמִיד תּוּקַד״ etc., end of quote. Regarding this particular detail he said חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס — meaning, for nothing, since there is on it none of the offerings, but only that the fire consume wood all night; and they too are equivalent to monetary loss." "<b>Further,</b> it may be seen that חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס is because the verse makes a great matter of the mitzvah of the daily olah, as it is written (Leviticus 6:5): ״וּבִעֵר עָלֶיהָ הַכֹּהֵן״ etc. ״וְעָרַךְ עָלֶיהָ הָעֹלָה״, and the Rabban taught in Torat Kohanim: From where do we know that nothing should precede the daily offering of the morning? Scripture says: ״עָלֶיהָ הָעֹלָה״, end of quote. And it is taught in the Tosefta of Pesaḥim: All offerings that were offered before the daily morning offering etc. are invalid etc., end of quote." "<b>Now</b> you have learned: if there is no olah, everything done before it is invalid. And from here many things that involve חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס cascade forth. Consider: he brought a lamb in its first year, and if he had offered it close to when it was brought it would have been valid; but because they say to him: wait until the daily morning offering is brought — they have caused him a loss by the delay of one hour, for hours are counted against consecrated animals, as is found in chapter 2 of Zevaḥim (Zevaḥim 25a) and in the chapter Elu Mumin (Bekhorot 39a)." "<b>And so</b> you find that if he performed the handful and the incense-burning before [its time], it is invalid. You also find that there could be a time when they had no sheep to offer — at a time when Jerusalem was under siege and there was no possibility of performing the service of the House of our Elohim to offer any of the offerings that come from other living creatures, nor meal-offerings, nor incense. And Israel needed to lay out a great sum of money for a sheep, because there was no possibility of offering until they were supplied in advance. And so you find (Bava Kamma 82b) that they would lower down to them a basket full of gold dinars for a single sheep, and you have no greater depletion of the treasury than this. And perhaps, were it not for the aforementioned constraint, they would not have given even half as much for a single sheep each day — and certainly not from the funds of the Most High." "<b>To say.</b> It is not known to whom it is said. And if to the generations, surely this matter is already heard from His saying ״צַו״ (Command), as we wrote nearby. And if to say it is said to Israel so that they would prepare the burnt offering and its appurtenances and the wood for the arrangement [on the altar], surely it already says ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ וגו׳ לֵאמֹר״." "<b>Or perhaps</b> — since He commanded him to say to Aaron the allusions hidden in the writing of Elohim, and the fine points that Scripture intends — it is because the verse is like a sealed vision. And what you find that our Sages of blessed memory derived is only according to what had previously reached them of the laws stated to Moses at Sinai, and those HaShem commanded לֵאמֹר, to say to Aaron." "<b>Or</b> it may intend: since disqualified offerings are accepted on the altar, even though they are disqualified as stated in their words (Torat Kohanim) in explaining the verse, therefore He commanded that he say to them that they should take care not to allow disqualified offerings to go up. For when they see that [such offerings] are not taken down, they might think that there is not such strict concern about their being offered, and they would not set their heart to guard them from going up. Therefore He commanded לֵאמֹר, to say to them, that they should ensure that nothing go up except תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה — meaning, a burnt offering according to its law and according to its proper procedure, with no overnight remaining [on the altar beyond its time], with no [unauthorized] removal [of the meat], and so forth:" "<b>זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה וגו׳.</b> Rabbi Yehuda derived (Torat Kohanim here) three exclusions: ״זֹאת״, ״הִיא״, and the second heh of ״הָעֹלָה״ — to exclude one slaughtered at night, one whose blood was spilled, and one whose blood went out beyond the curtains. These, if they went up [onto the altar], must be taken down. But one left overnight [linah], and an offering slaughtered with intent to eat it outside its proper time or place [piggul], and one that was impure [tameh], and so forth — if they went up, they are not taken down. And all other disqualifications, if they went up, are not taken down, for the word ״תּוֹרַת״ is a term of inclusion." "<b>And this</b> is the measure of the verse: ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה הִיא הָעֹלָה״ — meaning: anything that went up onto the pyre, etc., has the law of an olah. And you find no disqualification in it except before it went up; but once it went up onto the pyre on the altar, it has the law of an olah in every case, and the altar purifies all disqualifications except three things which are excluded as mentioned. For he should have said only ״תּוֹרַת הָעוֹלָה עוֹלָה עַל מוֹקְדָה״, yet he said ״זֹאת״, and he said ״הָא״ (i.e., הִיא), and he said ״הִיא״ — to exclude, as mentioned:" "<b>הִיא הָעֹלָה.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, wrote: it excludes one who had relations, etc. I do not know why he preferred to explain the verse according to Rabbi Shimon rather than according to Rabbi Yehudah, who excluded that which was slaughtered at night, and whose blood was spilled. Furthermore, the Rav did not formulate precisely in his language, for he wrote the exclusions not under ״זֹאת״ but under ״הִיא הָעֹלָה״, whereas Rabbi Shimon derives from ״זֹאת״." "<b>And it is straightforward to me</b> that from the exclusion of ״זֹאת״ alone does Rabbi Shimon derive, and not from ״הִיא הָעוֹלָה״. For Rabbi Shimon does not need many exclusions for the specific cases he stated regarding whether, if they went up, they come down, just as Rabbi Yehudah excluded three specific cases from three exclusions. For if we were to say so, we would need more exclusions than can be counted, since the specific cases that Rabbi Shimon mentioned, to which the exclusion applies, are numerous. Rather, it is certainly so: from a single exclusion he excludes everything — anything whose disqualification is not in the sacred domain, the sacred domain does not accept. And since only one exclusion is needed, and it is taught in the baraita (Zevaḥim 84a): ״תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר זֹאת״ — from where does Rashi get to say that he derives ״הִיא הָעוֹלָה״?" "<b>And you will find</b> that they said in Horayot (2a) regarding the mishnah that taught ״נֶפֶשׁ אַחַת״ etc. — these are exclusions: one who acts on his own initiative, etc. — and the Gemara establishes the baraita according to Rabbi Yehudah, and raises the objection: perhaps it is Rabbi Shimon? And it answers: whom have you heard who derives exclusions like this? Rabbi Yehudah — as it was taught: ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה הִוא הָעֹלָה״ — these are exclusions. This proves that Rabbi Shimon does not derive exclusions." "<b>עַל מוֹקְדָה עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ.</b> It will be explained according to what they said in the chapter HaMizbe'aḥ Mekaddesh (Zevaḥim 85b): all of these that if they went up, do not come down; if they came down, do not go up. Ulla said: they taught this only where the fire had not taken hold of them; but if the fire took hold of them — they go up. And although there is one who teaches the latter clause as referring to bones and sinews and horns, etc., the Gemara concludes that the dispute emerges only in the latter clause; but in the first clause — the limbs and the offerings that are disqualified which were subsumed under the olah, that if they went up, do not come down, and if they came down, do not go up — according to everyone, if the fire took hold of them, they go up even though they came down." "<b>And therefore</b> the verse says: concerning those over which the fire had already ruled, ״עַל מוֹקְדָה״, and concerning those over which the fire had not yet ruled but had gone up onto the altar, it says ״עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״. And the verse distinguishes between them because they differ in their laws: ״עַל מוֹקְדָה״ — it must be burned even if it was taken down; but what the verse adds by saying ״עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ — only if it went up and was not taken down." "<b>And therefore</b> the verse preceded to say ״עַל מוֹקְדָה״ before saying ״עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ — whereas it would seem proper to precede the ascent to the altar, since it does not become a burning except after the altar preceded. Rather, it intended to record the first stage, in which the disqualified offering becomes fully valid regarding the matter of ״if they were taken down they shall go up,״ and afterward it stated the second stage, in which there is no removal of disqualification — only as long as they are upon the altar, as mentioned." "<b>And in the Torah of</b> the priests, Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Yehoshua disagreed: Rabban Gamliel holds ״עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ — anything fit for the altar, if it went up it is not taken down, even libations; and Rabbi Yehoshua holds ״עַל מוֹקְדָה״ specifically, and not libations, etc. And there are seventy faces to the Torah (Zohar vol. I 47a)." "<b>And by way of</b> remez, the entire passage alludes to the final exile in which we find ourselves, to comfort us from the sorrow of our soul, for every Jewish man despairs of comfort upon seeing the length of the exile. To what may it be likened: to Egypt — four hundred years; to Babylon — seventy; for both together — four hundred and seventy; whereas today it is a thousand and [the Hebrew year] תרע״ב — what more can I hope for? And it is not exile alone but affliction from the nations, for every nation and kingdom subjugates Israel, and this constant cycle, mind you, is not good, for a language of truth has not been recognized with certainty while falsehood's tongue has grown long. And the Watcher who sees to the end of all generations came, calling the generations from the beginning, and informed Moses to rouse Israel — and at their head, the men of Torah, the lips of a priest who seeks Torah from his mouth: Aaron and his sons — to make this matter known to the generations." "<b>זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה</b> — the verse says ״זֹאת״ to exclude all the ascents that are not like this burnt-offering, and it goes back and explains which is this ״זֹאת,״ and says <b>הִוא הָעֹלָה</b> already, as it is written (Song of Songs 3:6) ״מִי זֹאת עוֹלָה מִן הַמִּדְבָּר״. ״עַל מוֹקְדָה עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ are two particulars that apply to us: one, that we are men of Torah — which is not so among all the nations; and the second, that we are chastened with exile and suffering and poverty. Concerning the Torah it says <b>עַל מוֹקְדָה</b>, for Torah is compared to fire, and so you will find that they said, of blessed memory (Ta'anit 4a): ״this fiery one is from the Rabbis, because the Torah burns within him,״ etc.; and concerning the exile and its branches it says <b>עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ</b>, for the sufferings are given the name מִזְבֵּחַ because they serve as atonement, and so it is in the language of the Sages (Berakhot 5b). And the verse informs us that through the combination of these two things, our ascent is destined with surpassing degrees of excellence — praises of praise that never were and never will be." "And it explains until when Israel will be within the category of these two things — <b>all the night</b>, which is the time of exile, likened to night: ״שׁוֹמֵר מַה מִּלַּיְלָה״ (Isaiah 21:11). And likewise in the verse ״לִינִי הַלַּיְלָה״ they, of blessed memory, expounded concerning the exile — until the morning, which is the time when His glory will be poured upon us, וְאָתָא בֹּקֶר. And the time is after five hundred [years] have passed into the sixth millennium, according to what has preceded for us from their words, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 19:8), that a day of the Holy One, blessed be He, is a thousand years. And from reason, the first five hundred [years] are the measure of night and the second five hundred the measure of day. And HaShem informed that until the morning — when five hundred years of exile are completed, until the morning when the ascent will be — this morning, I do not know whether it is the morning of the fifth millennium or the morning of the sixth millennium, for in the fourth millennium the glory of Israel went into exile in its wake. Therefore HaShem revealed His secret through His servants the prophets, that it is the second morning, which is what He said (Isaiah 33:2): ״הֱיֵה זְרֹעָם לַבְּקָרִים״ — for two mornings: for the second morning, if not for the first morning. And therefore He said ״עַד הַבֹּקֶר״ — the known morning, which is the second, and not the first morning that comes in the exile. And perhaps this is what the prophet hinted at in saying (Isaiah 21:12): ״אָמַר שׁוֹמֵר אָתָא בֹקֶר וְגַם לָיְלָה״ — meaning, even though the first morning came it availed nothing, and night also came after it, for we were not saved in it. And perhaps, had Israel been worthy, they would have been redeemed in the first morning." "<b>And this</b> is what is hinted in their words, that exile was decreed upon Israel for only one day, as it is written (Psalms 20:2): ״יַעַנְךָ ה׳ בְּיוֹם צָרָה״ — meaning, His day, of the Holy One, blessed be He. And there remain only 272 years, which are the end of the 400 [years] remaining from the fourth millennium and the 500 of the fifth millennium — totaling 272, and in the morning they would have been redeemed. But on account of their evil deeds He delayed and designated a second morning. And therefore He intended in saying (Isaiah 33:2): ״ה׳ חָנֵּנוּ לְךָ קִוִּינוּ״ etc. — meaning, we hoped to have the redemption in the first morning, so as not to delay until the second morning; but in any case, הֱיֵה זְרֹעָם לַבְּקָרִים, when the years pass." "And His saying <b>and the fire of the altar shall be kept burning in it</b> — the verse informs that in the light of morning the wrath of HaShem will smoke and fire from His mouth will consume, because the sons of iniquity afflicted us and chastised us with chastisements of vengeance. And especially the sons of the inner west — there is no bitter cup that they did not make us taste constantly. And this is His saying ״וְאֵשׁ הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״, in which we hinted the aspect of sufferings from the nations, and their hearts showed no mercy for [all that] HaShem had struck; [as if to say] this one has been struck for days, and the pride of Jacob has been humbled. And He did not conduct with them the aspect of deraron — to say, דַּי רְאוֹת, the vision of the supernal of supernal ones downward, downward. The fire of this altar shall be kept burning in it, in the hidden of the hidden, for He is the zealous One who clothes Himself in zeal. And He also hinted in saying ״וְאֵשׁ הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ to the aspect of the binding of Isaac. Also, the altar declares the aspect of judgment, for the judgments will be aroused and prevail — and this is the secret of His saying <b>תּוּקַד בּוֹ</b>; understand." "<b>And the priest shall put on his garment of linen.</b> It intends the attribute of kindness and mercy, for it is called by the name 'priest' — the intention being that even the [forces of] kindness will agree to take vengeance. And see what I explained in parashat Va'era etc., on the verse (Exodus 6:2): ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו אֲנִי ה׳״. And His saying ״מִדּוֹ בַד״ may be explained according to their saying, of blessed memory, that for every soul of Israel that the nations kill for the sanctification of His Name, blessed be He, the Holy One, blessed be He, impresses the form of that slain one's blood upon the garment, and that garment He will wear on the day of vengeance in His heart. And this is His saying מִדּוֹ אֲשֶׁר בּוֹ — those who are בַד, who are Israel, who are (Numbers 23:9): ״עָם לְבָדָד יִשְׁכֹּן״, and whose root is בַד. It also hints at the unity [for which] they are slain — because they did not wish to associate the Name of Heaven with anything else, and they unify His Name, blessed be He." "And His saying <b>and linen breeches</b> hints at the fact that the nations killed men who drew near to HaShem — those who instill faith in HaShem and His unity into the heart of Israel. And they are the dear ones of El Elyon, regarding whom nothing separates between Him and them. And this is what he was precise in saying ״וּמִכְנְסֵי בַד״ — in the language of instilling [הַכְנָסָה]. בַד is the aspect of unity. Regarding these He said ״עַל בְּשָׂרוֹ״ — meaning, nothing separates between them and HaShem. And He said <b>בְּשָׂרוֹ</b> to appease the ear [i.e., using anthropomorphic language familiar to the listener] — in the manner of (Jeremiah 13:11): ״כַּאֲשֶׁר יִדְבַּק הָאֵזוֹר אֶל מָתְנֵי אִישׁ״ etc.; (Numbers 11:1): ״בְּאָזְנֵי ה׳״; (Psalms 34:16): ״עֵינֵי ה׳״. And on account of what they did to these righteous ones, the decree will be sealed against the wicked nation." "And the verse's saying <b>and he shall lift up the ashes</b> corresponds to the evil they did in the aspect of sufferings and afflictions with which they oppressed us. And if you come to see, it is greater than the Egyptian exile, for in the Egyptian exile they would enslave them and feed them and clothe them — go out and learn from what they said, of blessed memory (Mekhilta, Yalkut Numbers 11:5) on the verse ״זָכַרְנוּ אֶת וגו׳ אֵת הַקִּשּׁוּאִים״ etc., that they ate everything for free. But the Ishmaelite exile — happy is the one who did not see them enslaving and embittering the lives of Israel. Not only do they not give wages, but they even demand of him, measure for measure, ״הָבָה״. Moreover, a man is normally robbed of what he has, but they demand of him what he does not have. And this cup they make him drink until he dies. And corresponding to this it says <b>and he shall lift up</b> — El lifts up my head — <b>the ashes which the fire consumes</b>, the fire of the nations, for we find that the distress of the nations is called fire; go out and learn from the fire among the cherubim (Ezekiel 10:2). <b>The burnt offering</b> — that is the nation called עוֹלָה." "<b>And the verse's saying</b> ״upon the altar״ will be explained by way of what Rambam (Hilkhot Teshuvah ch. 6) and Ra'avad disagreed about regarding the judgment of the idol worshippers who oppress our nation. For the Ra'avad holds that they are judged for the excess evil they did beyond what was decreed, since they possess free will and volition. And Rambam holds that they are judged even for the essence [of their actions] as well, and I have already written in Parashat Bein HaBetarim (Genesis 15:14) that the truth is that they are judged for everything, with good reason. And I speak something new, that from the excess one may draw proof regarding the essence: for if the intention of the oppressors had been to fulfill the command of HaShem, they should not have added anything, and from the fact that they added, they revealed their mind that they did not intend it as a commandment. And this is what the verse says ״upon the altar״ — meaning, beyond the measure for atonement that HaShem fixed for them — that they did more than the fixed amount; <b>and he shall place it beside the altar</b>, which is the fixed measure for atonement, and from this it will be judged that they do not intend the commandment of HaShem, for the excess reveals the aspect of the decree for atonement — whether they intend it [as divine command] or out of hatred merely to do evil, and they are found liable for everything. Or it may mean that even though the days of exile have not yet been completed, HaShem will come to the count of the remainder from the measure of distress that the children of wickedness added to afflict, and ״he shall place it beside the altar״ to complete through it the required atonement that HaShem fixed in the decree of exile. <b>Further,</b> it intends that He bring the deeds of the troubles before the attribute of justice, which is associated with the altar, in order to take vengeance from our enemies." "And the verse's saying <b>and he shall take off</b> etc. — for the first garments are for executing judgment upon those who do our evil, but now He comes to do good, to bring us out of exile and to benefit us; therefore He dons garments of the aspect of beneficence. For the ingathering of exiles will not be delayed until the evildoers are consumed, but rather immediately and at once He will gather our scattered ones and bring us out from the camp of the wicked men <b>to a pure place</b> — that is, the pure land of Israel, for the land of the nations is impure, both it and its dust and even its air (Shabbat 15a), and there is no place that may be called pure other than the Holy Land. It also alludes, in saying <b>a pure place</b>, to the place of the Holy One, blessed be He, Who is called pure, as it were." "And the verse's saying <b>and the fire upon the altar</b> — meaning: do not imagine that the only adversity awaiting the evildoers is the evil that HaShem will bring upon them through judgments when He brings us out from among them. Not so; rather, ״and the fire״ — the aspect of the fire of justice ״shall not go out״, <b>and the priest shall burn wood upon it</b> — they are the nations, who are dry wood, by way of what Scripture says (Deuteronomy 20:19) ״כִּי הָאָדָם עֵץ״, and so they said in Eikhah Rabbah (Introduction), and this is their language: ״the wood — these are the exiles,״ end quote." "And the verse's saying <b>morning by morning</b> intends, according to what we have prefaced, that the time of the redemption should properly have been the first morning of the year ת״ק of the fifth millennium, and on account of sin it was delayed until the second morning. And Scripture says that the nations will not merit escape because of this, nor will what was supposed to come upon them in the first morning be averted from them. Even if Israel did not merit redemption, the nations would still not escape the adversity that was destined to come upon them then, and HaShem will bring upon them what they incurred in the first morning and the second morning." "And the verse's saying <b>and he shall arrange upon it</b> etc. — this is explained according to the saying of the Sages, of blessed memory (Bava Kamma 83a): everything depends on the one who causes shame and the one who is shamed, for an honorable person who shames an honorable person is not comparable to one who shames a lowly person — and needless to say a slave who shames his master. And the verse says ״וְעָרַךְ עָלֶיהָ הָעֹלָה״ — that is, the congregation of Israel in its totality. <b>And he shall burn upon it</b> — meaning, He will also arrange the aspect of the perfect righteous ones, the select of Israel, the most perfect among them, who produce fragrance like spices, after the saying (Genesis 27:27): ״רְאֵה רֵיחַ בְּנִי כְּרֵיחַ שָׂדֶה״. And know that everyone who transgresses sins, his scent is foul, and the account brought in their words (Sefer Chasidim, chapter 7) is that Elijah once passed by and saw a wicked man and restrained himself from smelling his foul odor more than he felt when passing by the stench of a carcass; and the reverse is the case for a perfectly righteous man, from whom rises a perfume like the scent of the Garden. Therefore their memory of sweetness is mentioned in the language of burning (הקטרה), and the intent is that He will judge in particular detail those who did evil to men of fragrance. <b>Further</b>, it intends that when HaShem arranges the olah to take its vengeance from the evildoers, He will not arrange it on its own but will burn upon it the select and perfect ones of the nation, who will be called by the name ״חֵלֶב הַשְּׁלָמִים״ — for through this the merit of the nation to take great vengeance from its haters will be magnified." "And the verse's saying <b>a perpetual fire</b> etc. — meaning, even though HaShem will strike them a great blow, the fire will not be extinguished and will burn perpetually upon the altar, after the saying (Joel 4:21): ״וְנִקֵּיתִי דָּמָם לֹא נִקֵּיתִי״, until the spirit of impurity is obliterated from the earth, and HaShem will be King over all the earth (Zechariah 14:9)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {9 "The phrase וְזֶבַח הַיּוֹצֵא refers to piggul, an offering rendered invalid due to intent to consume it outside its permitted time or place.", 13 "The text ״אין דריש מיעוט״ appears elliptical; read hyper-literally as 'does not derive exclusions,' i.e., from multiple exclusionary terms in this verse.", 18 "The year תרע״ב (5572 from creation) corresponds to 1812 CE; Or HaChaim completed his commentary in the early 1740s, so this likely reflects a copyist's later annotation or a variant reading of the numeral. Translating ״שְׂפַת אֱמֶת״ literally; the phrase appears deliberately broken (reading לֹא הֻכְּרָה rather than the expected idiom) and may carry a wordplay on ״כּוֹנְנוּת / שֶׁקֶר.״", 19 "The Aramaic phrase הַאי צוּרְבָּא מֵרַבָּנָן דְּרָתַח אוֹרַיְתָא is rendered literally; the original (Ta'anit 4a) reads מֵחַבְרַיָּא and דְּלִבֵּיהּ רָתַח אוֹרַיְתָא — the textual variants here may stem from Or HaChaim's citation from memory or from the printing house.", 21 "The gematria תרע״ב (272) depends on ע״ק as an abbreviation for 400; possibly a variant or scribal form of ת״ק. The arithmetic 400 + 500 = 900, not 272, so the precise logic of the calculation as printed is unclear; translated faithfully as it stands.", 22 "The phrase בְּחִינַת דֵּרָאוֹן is obscure; read hyper-literally as 'the aspect of deraron,' possibly a kabbalistic term of art, with the following wordplay on דַּי רְאוֹת rendered as given.", 25 "The author reads the priestly ash-removal ritual as an allegory for divine redress for the nations' oppression of Israel in exile.", 30 "The citation '(קב הישר פרק ז)' refers to Sefer Chasidim; resolved by context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:57.973155796Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}